#VRML_SIM R2025a utf8

# A GRIPPER HOLDING CLOTH. No arm, no IK -- a two-jaw gripper on a flying
# prismatic gantry closes on a hanging sheet and lifts it.
#
# ⚠ THIS WORLD EXISTS TO EXERCISE ONE FIELD: WorldInfo.newtonSolver "vbd".
# It is the first world in the tree where a rigid body and a cloth particle are
# solved by the SAME solver in the SAME sweep. Everything else about it is
# ordinary; that one line is the point.
#
# WHY NOT "mujoco+vbd", WHICH IS THE CLOTH DEFAULT. On the coupled path the
# cloth lives on SolverVBD and the rigid world on SolverMuJoCo, exchanged by a
# SolverCoupledProxy. The fabric therefore only ever sees a jaw whose pose was
# synced from the PREVIOUS rigid solve, so a pinch is always resolved against a
# one-step-stale pad. newton's own cloth-grasp example
# (examples/vbd/example_vbd_gripper_soft_grid.py) puts gripper AND grid on one
# SolverVBD for exactly that reason, and it is the only configuration in the
# package where a rigid gripper lifts a soft mesh. Before newtonSolver "vbd"
# existed, that configuration could not be written as an OmniSim world at all:
# the single-SolverVBD branch in the runtime was reachable only by a world with
# literally zero rigid bodies.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, because it is not free and the log will tell you so at load:
# there is no mj_model on this path, so raycast-backed devices, Connector /
# VacuumGripper welds, TouchSensor and the MuJoCo contact readback are gone;
# rigid contact is AVBD rather than MuJoCo's friction cone; and newton silently
# ignores joint armature, joint friction, effort limits, velocity limits and
# target_mode. The engine logs one line per feature THIS world actually uses.
#
# ⚠ CONSEQUENCE FOR THE MOTORS BELOW, and it looks like a mistake if you do not
# know it: maxForce here is NOT a force ceiling. OmniSim turns a position
# motor's maxForce into the joint's PD stiffness (targetKe = maxForce * 10,
# targetKd = maxForce * 0.5), and VBD honours those gains while ignoring the
# effort limit itself. So maxForce 10000 on a jaw means "ke = 1e5", which is
# newton's own finger_drive_ke from the example -- it does not mean the jaw can
# push with 10 kN. Read these numbers as stiffnesses.
#
# THE SCENE. A 0.40 x 0.36 m sheet (20 x 18 cells at 20 mm, 399 particles,
# 0.10 kg total) hangs in the world X-Z plane, pinned along its top edge at
# z = 0.90. The gripper starts 0.30 m to the +X side at the grasp height,
# flies in along -X, closes on the sheet face, and lifts 0.25 m.
#
# HOW A HOLD IS PROVEN, and why the pinned top edge does not confound it. The
# measurement is (rise of the gripped region) - (rise of the jaws) over the
# lift. A pinned edge can only ever prevent the fabric from FALLING; it cannot
# lift the fabric. So a gripped region that rises WITH the jaws has only one
# available explanation. The negative control (VBD_GRASP_NEGATIVE=1: identical
# flight path, jaws never close) makes that concrete -- there the sheet stays
# hanging while the jaws rise, and the same number reads about -0.25 m.
# ⚠ Stated plainly rather than dressed up: this does NOT prove the grip carries
# the sheet's whole weight the way an unpin would. OmniSim's Cloth has no
# runtime unpin, so that half of the standalone proof is not reproduced here.
# By the end of the lift the grip point sits ~10 mm ABOVE the pinned edge, so
# the fabric is draped over the jaws and much of the sheet does hang from them,
# but "much" is not "all" and the number below is a TRACKING number, not a
# load-bearing one.
#
# MEASURING IT. The cloth has no supervisor accessor -- particles are not scene
# nodes -- so the fabric is read through the engine's own telemetry:
#   OMNISIM_CLOTH_TELEMETRY=<path.jsonl> OMNISIM_CLOTH_TELEMETRY_FULL=1
#   OMNISIM_CLOTH_TELEMETRY_EVERY=1
# and the controller writes the matching pad poses to VBD_GRASP_LOG.
#
# ==========================================================================
# PARTICLE SELF-CONTACT IS OFF HERE (`newtonClothSelfContact 0`), OR THE GRIP
# SLIPS 24x. The world says so itself -- it was env-var-ONLY until 2026-08-15,
# and a forgotten variable did not fail, it slipped, silently.
# ==========================================================================
# THIS IS THE ONE THING TO KNOW ABOUT THIS WORLD, and it is a genuine collision
# between two defaults that are each individually right.
#
# `particle_enable_self_contact` is ON by default in OmniSim (env
# OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT), and correctly so: newton's default is OFF, and
# with it OFF a fold passes straight through itself, which is wrong for the
# draping sheet that is cloth's normal case. But a PINCH is the opposite case.
# Fabric gathered inside the jaws self-collides, and that self-collision pushes
# the fabric back out of the pinch. newton's own gripper example does not enable
# it, and the standalone reproduction this world is based on did not either.
#
# MEASURED HERE, same world, same binary, same machine (RTX 3060 laptop,
# newton 1.5.0 / warp 1.16.0 / cuda:0), one run per row, n=1:
#
#   run                              tracking err   slip max   held_min_z
#   self-contact ON  (the default)     -22.11 mm    37.89 mm     0.7305
#   self-contact OFF                    -0.92 mm     2.76 mm     0.7378
#   NEG-CTRL, self-contact OFF        -249.67 mm   249.81 mm     0.5397
#
# tracking err = (rise of the gripped 9-particle region) - (rise of the measured
# jaw midpoint) between the end of `preload` and the end of `hold`, over a
# ~250 mm lift. Both instants sit deep inside a static window, and the analysis
# re-runs the number at cloth-telemetry offsets of +/-8 steps to show it is
# alignment-insensitive (-0.93 .. -0.91 mm across that sweep).
# held_min_z = lowest particle at the end of the hold; the floor top is z = 0.
# The negative control's sheet never leaves its rest pose (0.5397..0.9000) while
# the jaws rise 250 mm -- so the 248.64 mm the fabric DOES rise in the grasping
# case is attributable to the pinch and to nothing else in the scene.
# Peak per-body soft contacts was 467 against the 2048 buffer, i.e. no overflow
# -- but well past newton's own 256 default, which this runtime already raises.
#
# ✅ FIXED 2026-08-15: `newtonClothSelfContact 0` in WorldInfo below. Until
# then there was no world FIELD for this, only the env var, so opening this
# demo in the GUI with nothing set ran the -22 mm row above -- still a hold,
# still a lift, just a visibly sloppier one, with nothing to say why. The env
# var still overrides (env > field > default), so the ON row remains
# reproducible without editing the file.
#
# ⚠ CLOTH IS A GPU WORKLOAD and this world is denser than newton_cloth_drape
# (399 particles vs 289, plus a 5-DoF articulation in the same solver). It
# finalizes on CUDA by design -- see the model-device note in the runtime.
# Wall clock, this machine: ~6.95 s of sim time takes ~35 s.

EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/backgrounds/protos/OmniSimSky.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSun.proto"
EXTERNPROTO "omnisim://projects/objects/lights/protos/OmniSimSunMarker.proto"

WorldInfo {
  gravity 9.81
  basicTimeStep 8
  coordinateSystem "ENU"
  # THE FIELD THIS WORLD IS FOR. One SolverVBD owns the gantry, the jaws and
  # every cloth particle. Read the field's note in resources/nodes/WorldInfo.wrl
  # before copying this into a world that also needs sensors or welds.
  newtonSolver "vbd"
  # 8 ms / 10 = 800 us substeps. newton's gripper example runs 833 us (60 fps /
  # 20) and its fragility notes measure the pinch degrading GRACEFULLY rather
  # than cliffing as this coarsens: 20 substeps gave -0.18 mm tracking and 4
  # gave -2.69 mm, still a hold. So this is a quality knob, not a cliff.
  newtonSubsteps 10
  # Without this the floor Solid below is not a collider, and the failure case
  # (a dropped sheet) would fall through the world instead of landing.
  newtonStatics TRUE
  # Sets the shape-side friction. The cloth's particle-side mu defaults to this
  # same value, and newton takes the GEOMETRIC MEAN of the two, so the effective
  # pad-fabric friction is 1.0 -- newton's own example value for this grasp.
  newtonGroundMu 1
  # OFF because this world PINCHES. See the header: the 24x is measured here.
  # A draping world wants the opposite and should leave this unset (the
  # runtime default is ON). OMNISIM_CLOTH_SELF_CONTACT still overrides.
  newtonClothSelfContact 0
}
Viewpoint {
  position 1.817 -1.934 2.419
  orientation -0.278945 0.120681 0.952694 2.359689
}
OmniSimSky {
}
DEF SUN OmniSimSun {
}
DEF SUN_MARKER OmniSimSunMarker {
}
# Top face at z = 0. Only ever touched by a FAILED grasp -- it is the thing that
# makes "the sheet is on the floor" a measurable outcome rather than a fall
# through the world.
DEF FLOOR Solid {
  translation 0 0 -0.05
  children [
    Shape {
      appearance PBRAppearance {
        baseColor 0.33 0.34 0.38
        roughness 1
        metalness 0
      }
      geometry Box {
        size 3 3 0.1
      }
    }
  ]
  name "floor"
  boundingObject Box {
    size 3 3 0.1
  }
}
# 21 x 19 = 399 particles. mass is PER PARTICLE (see Cloth.wrl), so 0.10 kg over
# the whole sheet is 0.10 / 399 = 2.506e-4 each. That is 0.69 kg/m^2 -- a towel.
# ⚠ Sheet mass was never swept in the standalone proof this world reproduces;
# it is the single most obvious way to break the grasp and it is untested.
#
# The patch is authored flat in its own local Z = 0 plane and stood up by the
# rotation: +90 deg about X maps local (x, y, 0) -> world (x, 0, y), so the
# sheet spans world X and Z with its normal along Y -- which is the axis the
# jaws close along, as a parallel-jaw pinch on a sheet requires. fixTop pins the
# local +Y edge, which that rotation has made the world TOP edge at z = 0.90.
DEF SHEET Cloth {
  translation -0.2 0 0.54
  rotation 1 0 0 1.5707963
  dimX 20
  dimY 18
  cellX 0.02
  cellY 0.02
  mass 0.000250627
  # 5 mm collision shell, well under half the 20 mm cell so self-collision does
  # not fight the mesh. It is also half of the pinch geometry: the jaws close to
  # (pad_half_thickness + particleRadius - 2 mm), so this value sets where the
  # pads have to stop.
  particleRadius 0.005
  # Membrane stretch / area / damping and near-zero bending, so the sheet drapes
  # like fabric instead of behaving like a plate. triKe/triKa/triKd are newton's
  # example values; edgeKe 1e-3 is a deliberate "let it drape" choice.
  triKe 500
  triKa 500
  triKd 0.1
  edgeKe 0.001
  fixTop TRUE
  diffuseColor 0.85 0.52 0.22
  # ⚠ PURELY COSMETIC -- NOT ONE PHYSICAL FIELD IS TOUCHED BY THIS BLOCK, so
  # every measured number in the header above still stands. `appearance` is read
  # by the renderer and nothing else; the particles do not know it is here.
  #
  # ⚠ WHAT IT BUYS: the sheet was 6.43% of the frame PURE BLACK and is now
  # 0.00% (harness, 300 basic steps, the framing below, 1900x1080). The
  # canonical recipe declares `ambientIntensity 0` (OmniSimSun.proto), so ALL
  # fill light in an OmniSim world is image-based, and image-based light reaches
  # a surface ONLY through a PBRAppearance. This sheet stands in the X-Z plane
  # with its normal along Y, i.e. edge-on to a sun aimed (-0.5, -0.5, -0.3), so
  # under Phong one whole face of it rendered at exactly (0,0,0) -- and, because
  # a back face is shaded with the FRONT face's normal, the boundary between the
  # two came out as a hard per-triangle sawtooth rather than a shaded falloff.
  # A world whose entire subject is a sheet of fabric could not afford that.
  #
  # THE MAPS. `woven_cotton_*` from scripts/dev/gen_surface_textures.py: plain
  # 1/1 weave, 48 threads per 2 cm design tile.
  #   ⚠ baseColorMap is MULTIPLIED by `baseColor` (pbr.frag:259), not replaced.
  #     The woven set is generated near-neutral (0.82 0.815 0.80 linear) exactly
  #     so a world can tint it here, which is how this sheet keeps the tan it
  #     had as a Phong `diffuseColor`.
  #   ⚠ metalness 0 EXPLICITLY. The schema default is 1.
  #   ⚠ THE TILE IS 8 CM, matching every other cloth world in this directory so
  #     the four read as the same bolt of fabric: `scale 5 4.5` over 0.40 x 0.36
  #     m is 8.0 x 8.0 cm. That is 4x the map's 2 cm design size, and the
  #     honest 2 cm was swept and rejected on evidence -- see the tile table in
  #     newton_cloth_drape.omniworld, where a 2 cm tile renders COMPLETELY FLAT
  #     because a 0.42 mm thread is far under one pixel at demo framing.
  #   ⚠ normalMapFactor 0.5 pays for that 4x stretch (the map's median tilt is
  #     48.6 deg, aggressive even unstretched).
  #   The normal map's green channel is DOWN (-Y / "DirectX"), which is what
  #   this engine's v-increases-downward texture upload requires. Never
  #   substitute a stock OpenGL/+Y map: every thread would light as a groove.
  appearance PBRAppearance {
    baseColor 0.80 0.46 0.18
    baseColorMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_basecolor.png"
      ]
    }
    normalMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_normal.png"
      ]
    }
    roughnessMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_roughness.png"
      ]
    }
    occlusionMap ImageTexture {
      url [
        "textures/woven_cotton_occlusion.png"
      ]
    }
    metalness 0
    normalMapFactor 0.5
    IBLStrength 1.3
    textureTransform TextureTransform {
      scale 5 4.5
    }
  }
}
# The flying gripper. The root Robot declares NO physics, so it is a STATIC
# anchor and the gantry hangs off it -- which also keeps the articulation free
# of a FREE joint, and that matters more than it looks:
# ⚠ VBD reads a position target at joint_target_q[q_index] while OmniSim writes
# it at the qd index. Those coincide for an articulation of 1-DoF joints and
# DIVERGE the moment a 7-q / 6-qd FREE joint precedes them in the model. Keep
# newtonSolver "vbd" articulations rooted on a static base until that is fixed.
#
# Three world-axis prismatic DoF carry two jaws that slide along +/-Y. The
# intermediate links carry 1 mm sphere colliders rather than none: a Newton body
# with no resolvable boundingObject is given a 12 cm sphere as a fallback, which
# here would be a hidden ball swiping through the sheet.
DEF GANTRY Robot {
  translation 0.3 0 0.66
  children [
    DEF GX SliderJoint {
      jointParameters JointParameters {
        axis 1 0 0
      }
      device [
        LinearMotor {
          name "gantry_x"
          maxForce 5000
          maxVelocity 5
          minPosition -1
          maxPosition 1
        }
      ]
      endPoint DEF BASE_X Solid {
        children [
          DEF GY SliderJoint {
            jointParameters JointParameters {
              axis 0 1 0
            }
            device [
              LinearMotor {
                name "gantry_y"
                maxForce 5000
                maxVelocity 5
                minPosition -1
                maxPosition 1
              }
            ]
            endPoint DEF BASE_Y Solid {
              children [
                DEF GZ SliderJoint {
                  jointParameters JointParameters {
                    axis 0 0 1
                  }
                  device [
                    LinearMotor {
                      name "gantry_z"
                      maxForce 5000
                      maxVelocity 5
                      minPosition -1
                      maxPosition 1
                    }
                  ]
                  endPoint DEF CARRIAGE Solid {
                    children [
                      # Jaw joint zero == OPEN. Both jaws take the SAME command
                      # (how far closed from open) because their axes point at
                      # each other, which keeps the pinch symmetric by
                      # construction instead of by two matching sign flips.
                      DEF JL SliderJoint {
                        jointParameters JointParameters {
                          axis 0 1 0
                        }
                        device [
                          LinearMotor {
                            name "jaw_left"
                            maxForce 10000
                            maxVelocity 5
                            minPosition -0.14
                            maxPosition 0.05
                          }
                        ]
                        endPoint DEF LEFT_PAD Solid {
                          translation 0 -0.053 0
                          children [
                            Shape {
                              appearance PBRAppearance {
                                baseColor 0.85 0.3 0.25
                                roughness 0.8
                                metalness 0
                              }
                              geometry Box {
                                size 0.05 0.01 0.05
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                          name "left_pad"
                          boundingObject Box {
                            size 0.05 0.01 0.05
                          }
                          physics Physics {
                            density -1
                            mass 0.025
                          }
                        }
                      }
                      DEF JR SliderJoint {
                        jointParameters JointParameters {
                          axis 0 -1 0
                        }
                        device [
                          LinearMotor {
                            name "jaw_right"
                            maxForce 10000
                            maxVelocity 5
                            minPosition -0.14
                            maxPosition 0.05
                          }
                        ]
                        endPoint DEF RIGHT_PAD Solid {
                          translation 0 0.053 0
                          children [
                            Shape {
                              appearance PBRAppearance {
                                baseColor 0.25 0.35 0.85
                                roughness 0.8
                                metalness 0
                              }
                              geometry Box {
                                size 0.05 0.01 0.05
                              }
                            }
                          ]
                          name "right_pad"
                          boundingObject Box {
                            size 0.05 0.01 0.05
                          }
                          physics Physics {
                            density -1
                            mass 0.025
                          }
                        }
                      }
                    ]
                    name "carriage"
                    boundingObject Sphere {
                      radius 0.001
                    }
                    physics Physics {
                      density -1
                      mass 0.01
                    }
                  }
                }
              ]
              name "base_y"
              boundingObject Sphere {
                radius 0.001
              }
              physics Physics {
                density -1
                mass 0.01
              }
            }
          }
        ]
        name "base_x"
        boundingObject Sphere {
          radius 0.001
        }
        physics Physics {
          density -1
          mass 0.01
        }
      }
    }
  ]
  name "gantry"
  controller "vbd_cloth_grasp"
  supervisor TRUE
}
